      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The majority's reasoning is grounded in the constitutional principle of the \"independent Article III judiciary,\" asserting that federal courts must not simply defer to the executive branch but must exercise their own judgment on all statutory questions. While a strong scholarly consensus exists that the *pre-*Loper Bright era had distorted the original understanding of Chevron, the majority argues this historical practice was a \"de facto\" re-creation of the \"unrestrained discretion\" that the Constitution was designed to prevent. Rather than being an ideological shift, the decision reframes legal interpretation as a singular exercise of judicial authority rather than a predictable, albeit biased, delegation of power to administrators. The opinion relies heavily on the original meaning of the \"one power to the people\" to argue that the administrative state has always been subject to direct review, not the deferential \"catch-all\" the dissent and scholars claim it mistakenly became.",
